Albert Marquet’s 1911 oil painting "Les Deux Amies" depicts an 
intimate indoor scene with two nude women in black stockings, 
showcasing Marquet’s transition from Fauvism to a more tempered 
impressionist style.

Marquet’s work captures both the intimacy of private life 
and an experimental approach to figure painting, blending 
elements of eroticism and formal composition. The painting 
has been reproduced and studied extensively in art 
historical literature, reflecting its importance in 
understanding Marquet’s evolving stylistic approach 

The imagery emphasizes the sensuality of human connection, 
depicted with the subtlety of early 20th-century French 
painting, making it a key work for studies of Marquet’s 
exploration of female nudes and domestic interiors 

Visual Description

The painting portrays two women in an intimate setting: one is 
reclining on a divan, lying on her back, while the other sits 
at the base of the divan on the floor. Both women are depicted 
wearing only black stockings, highlighting Marquet’s focus on 
form, contour, and the subtle interplay of light and shadow on 
flesh 

. The indoor scene emphasizes a private, delicate interaction 
between the figures, rendered with restrained color and soft 
oil brushwork characteristic of Marquet’s style in the early 
1910s 

Technique and Dimensions

Medium: Oil on canvas

Dimensions: 60 × 92 cm

Artistic Approach: Marquet employs a careful composition of 
lines and curves that emphasizes the intimacy and grace of 
the figures, using warm tones for the skin and contrasting 
dark hues for the stockings and divan surface 

Historical Context

"Les Deux Amies" was executed amid Marquet’s transition from
Fauvism to a more nuanced impressionist approach, blending 
expressive line and color with observational subtlety. It is
notable within his oeuvre for the erotic yet tender
depiction of the female nude, which is relatively rare for 
his catalog 

Ministère de la Culture

. Early exhibition references include a 1937 Paris 
exhibition where it was titled "Les deux femmes" 

Provenance and Current Location

Acquisition: Donated by Adèle and George Besson in 1963

Current Status: The painting is on deposit since 1972 at 
the Musée des Beaux-Arts et d’Archéologie de Besançon, 
France, part of the French national collections managed by 
the Centre Pompidou 
